            [
                'title' => 'Understanding CAP Rounds in Maharashtra: A Step-by-Step Guide for Engineering Admissions',
                'category' => 'Admissions & Counselling',
                'tags' => ['CAP Rounds', 'Admissions', 'MHT CET', 'Counselling', 'Maharashtra'],
                'author' => 'CareerGyan Admissions Team',
                'content' => '<h2>Introduction to CAP Rounds</h2>
<p>The Centralized Admission Process (CAP) is the official mechanism through which the State Common Entrance Test Cell, Maharashtra, conducts admissions for first-year undergraduate engineering (B.E./B.Tech) courses. For many students and parents, the CAP round process can seem daunting and confusing. The fear of making a mistake in the option form and losing out on a good college is very real.</p>
<p>In this detailed guide, we will simplify the entire CAP round procedure, explaining each step from registration to final admission, so you can navigate the process with confidence and secure the best possible college based on your MHT CET or JEE Main scores.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Online Registration and Document Verification</h2>
<p>The CAP process officially begins after the declaration of MHT CET results. The CET Cell releases an official notification with the schedule.</p>
<p>First, candidates must register online on the official CET Cell portal. During registration, you will need to fill in your personal details, academic qualifications, and entrance exam scores. You will also need to upload scanned copies of required documents such as your 10th and 12th mark sheets, domicile certificate, category certificate (if applicable), and income certificate.</p>
<p>After online registration, your documents must be verified. Previously, this required physical visits to Facilitation Centres (FCs), but the CET Cell now utilizes an <strong>e-Scrutiny</strong> system. Your uploaded documents will be verified online by the authorities. Once verified, you will receive an acknowledgment receipt.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Display of Provisional and Final Merit Lists</h2>
<p>Following document verification, the CET Cell publishes the <strong>Provisional Merit List</strong>. This list displays your State General Merit Number, Category Merit Number, and University Merit Number based on your percentile.</p>
<p>It is crucial to check this list carefully. If you find any discrepancies (e.g., incorrect marks, wrong category name), you are given a specific window to submit grievances online. After all grievances are resolved, the <strong>Final Merit List</strong> is published. Your rank in this final list is what dictates your admission chances.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Option Form Filling (The Most Crucial Step)</h2>
<p>This is where your strategy comes into play. Before CAP Round 1, the CET Cell publishes the Seat Matrix, which details the number of available seats in every college, branch, and category.</p>
<p>You must fill out the online Option Form, where you list your preferred colleges and branches in decreasing order of priority. You can fill a minimum of 1 and a maximum of 300 choices. <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Always list your true dream colleges at the very top (Options 1 to 5), even if your percentile is slightly lower than their historical cutoffs. The algorithm assigns seats by checking your choices strictly from top to bottom.</p>
<h2>Step 4: CAP Round Allotment and Freeze/Float/Slide Options</h2>
<p>Based on your merit rank and the choices you filled, the computer algorithm will allot you a seat. When the allotment results are declared, you generally have three choices:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Auto-Freeze:</strong> If you are allotted your <em>First Preference</em> (Option No. 1), it is automatically frozen. You must accept this seat, pay the seat acceptance fee, and report to the college. You cannot participate in further CAP rounds.</li>
<li><strong>Self-Freeze:</strong> If you are allotted an option other than your first preference, but you are completely satisfied with it, you can manually "Freeze" the seat. You pay the acceptance fee, secure admission in that college, and exit the CAP process.</li>
<li><strong>Betterment (Float):</strong> If you are allotted a seat but want to try for a better option in the next round, you choose "Betterment" or "Float". You must pay the seat acceptance fee to hold your current seat, but you remain eligible for the next CAP round. If a better seat is allotted in the next round, your previous seat is automatically cancelled. If no better seat is allotted, you retain your previous seat.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 5: Subsequent CAP Rounds and Institute Level Quota</h2>
<p>Usually, there are three CAP rounds. Before Round 2 and Round 3, vacant seats are displayed, and you can edit your option form. Only fill those options in Round 2 that are genuinely better than your currently held seat.</p>
<p>After all CAP rounds are concluded, some seats may remain vacant. Colleges conduct <strong>Spot Rounds</strong> or Institute Level Counseling to fill these vacancies. Additionally, private unaided colleges have a 20% Institute Level (Management) Quota, which is filled directly by the colleges at the institutional level based on merit.</p>
<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Putting safe colleges at the top:</strong> Never put a lower-tier college as Option 1 just because you are sure to get it. If allotted, it will be auto-frozen.</li>
<li><strong>Not checking category cutoffs:</strong> Look at the cutoffs for your specific category (OBC, SC, ST, EWS, TFWS) rather than just the general open cutoffs.</li>
<li><strong>Missing deadlines:</strong> The CAP schedule is extremely strict. Missing the deadline for document verification or seat acceptance fee payment will knock you out of the process.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>The CAP round process is highly logical and transparent. By understanding the rules of Freezing and Floating, researching historical cutoffs, and carefully designing your option form, you can maximize your chances of getting into the best possible engineering college. Utilize tools like the CareerGyan MHT CET Cutoff Explorer to research past trends and make informed decisions.</p>'
